.\" cligPart NAME
.SH NAME
coherentsearch \- Search normalised FFTs for pulsars by coherently summing many harmonics and scoring the folded profiles with boxcar matched filters.
.\" cligPart NAME end

.\" cligPart SYNOPSIS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coherentsearch
[-threshold threshold]
[-nharms nharms]
[-maxdecim maxdecim]
[-lofreq lofreq]
[-lobin lobin]
[-hifreq hifreq]
[-hidr hidr]
[-m m]
[-measuredsigma]
[-ncands ncands]
[-drtol drtol]
[-numharm numharm]
[-noremove]
[-noharmremove]
[-blocksize blocksize]
[-o outfile]
[-outdir outdir]
[-noprogress]
[-verbose]
[--] infile ...
.\" cligPart SYNOPSIS end

.\" cligPart OPTIONS
.SH OPTIONS
.IP -threshold
S/N cutoff for candidates,
.br
1 Double value between 0.0 and oo.
.br
Default: `8.0'
.IP -nharms
Number of harmonics to sum coherently (60 is divisible by 2-6, so it decimates evenly),
.br
1 Int value between 2 and 1024.
.br
Default: `60'
.IP -maxdecim
Largest harmonic-decimation factor k: also fold nharms/k harmonics at k times each fundamental (1 disables it),
.br
1 Int value between 1 and 64.
.br
Default: `6'
.IP -lofreq
Lowest fundamental frequency (Hz) to search,
.br
1 Double value between 0.0 and oo.
.br
Default: `0.1'
.IP -lobin
Lowest Fourier bin to search (overrides -lofreq),
.br
1 Int value between 0 and oo.
.IP -hifreq
Highest fundamental frequency (Hz) to search.  Decimation extends coverage to hifreq * maxdecim,
.br
1 Double value between 0.0 and oo.
.br
Default: `125.0'
.IP -hidr
Trial spacing (Fourier bins) at the highest harmonic,
.br
1 Double value between 0.0 and oo.
.br
Default: `0.5'
.IP -m
Fourier bins in the interpolation kernel (even),
.br
1 Int value between 2 and 1024.
.br
Default: `16'
.IP -measuredsigma
Measure each chunk's noise scale (robust MAD) instead of computing it from a normalised FFT.
.IP -ncands
Maximum number of candidates to report,
.br
1 Int value between 1 and oo.
.br
Default: `100'
.IP -drtol
Fourier-bin tolerance for collapsing near-identical candidates,
.br
1 Double value between 0.0 and oo.
.br
Default: `1.0'
.IP -numharm
Highest harmonic number when collapsing harmonically related candidates,
.br
1 Int value between 1 and 1024.
.br
Default: `16'
.IP -noremove
Do not collapse near-identical candidates.
.IP -noharmremove
Do not collapse harmonically related candidates.
.IP -blocksize
Trial fundamentals per chunk,
.br
1 Int value between 1 and oo.
.br
Default: `2048'
.IP -o
Output candidate file name (single input file only; default: <input>.cohout beside it),
.br
1 String value
.IP -outdir
Write each <input>.cohout into this directory instead,
.br
1 String value
.IP -noprogress
Do not print a progress meter.
.IP -verbose
Report the trial grid and chunking before searching.
.IP infile
Input '.fft' file(s), each with a matching '.inf' file.  They should be normalised (e.g. by rednoise) so that Fourier powers have mean 1.
.\" cligPart OPTIONS end

.\" cligPart DESCRIPTION
.SH DESCRIPTION
coherentsearch searches normalised Fourier power spectra
.RI ( .fft
files, normally from
.BR realfft (1)
followed by
.BR rednoise (1))
for periodic signals.  Unlike
.BR accelsearch (1),
which adds harmonic
.I powers
incoherently, it interpolates the complex amplitude of every harmonic
exactly, inverse-FFTs the harmonic stack into a pulse profile, and
scores that profile with a bank of boxcar matched filters.  Keeping the
phases makes it most sensitive to narrow pulses, which put their power
into many harmonics.  It is a C port of the production search in
CoherentSearch.jl (Ransom 2026, arXiv:2610.11749).
.PP
Every trial fundamental, spaced so that the highest harmonic moves by
.B -hidr
bins, is folded with
.B -nharms
harmonics.  Harmonic decimation then re-uses the same amplitudes to fold
every k-th harmonic
.RB ( "k = 2 .. -maxdecim" )
at k times each fundamental, almost for free.  The profile S/N is
normalised by a noise scale computed from the number of harmonics
summed, which assumes Fourier powers of mean 1; a check on a few chunks
warns if the data disagree, and
.B -measuredsigma
estimates the scale from the data instead.
.PP
Candidates above
.B -threshold
are collapsed (near-identical frequencies, then harmonically related
ones), and the strongest
.B -ncands
are written to
.IR <input>.cohout ,
with each one's S/N, frequency, period, number of harmonics summed and
best-matching duty cycle.
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
realfft mypulsar_DM10.00.dat
rednoise mypulsar_DM10.00.fft
coherentsearch mypulsar_DM10.00_red.fft
.fi
.RE

.\" cligPart DESCRIPTION end

